EN = {
0: "In a certain department... but it is better not to say which department. There is nothing touchier than all sorts of departments, regiments, chancelleries and, in a word, every kind of official body. Nowadays every private person takes the whole of society to be insulted in his own person. They say that quite recently a petition came in from a certain police captain, I don't remember of what town, in which he sets out clearly that the institutions of the state are going to ruin and that his sacred name is being taken quite in vain. And as proof he enclosed with the petition an enormous volume of some romantic novel in which, every ten pages, a police captain appears, in places even completely drunk. So, to avoid any unpleasantness, we had better call the department in question simply “a certain department.”",
1: "So, in a certain department there served a certain clerk. A clerk not, one could say, very remarkable: short of stature, somewhat pockmarked, somewhat red-haired, even somewhat weak-sighted to look at, with a small bald patch on his forehead, wrinkles on both cheeks and a complexion of the kind called haemorrhoidal... What can one do! The Petersburg climate is to blame. As for his rank (for with us the rank must be announced before anything else), he was what is called a perpetual titular councillor, the sort at whom, as is well known, various writers have jeered and sharpened their wit to their hearts' content, having the praiseworthy habit of leaning hard on those who cannot bite back. The clerk's surname was Bashmachkin. It is plain from the name itself that it once came from “bashmak,” a shoe; but when, at what time and in what manner it came from a shoe, nothing of this is known.",
2: "Father, grandfather, even brother-in-law and absolutely all the Bashmachkins went about in boots, merely changing the soles three times a year. His name was Akaky Akakievich. Perhaps the reader will find it somewhat strange and far-fetched, but I can assure him that it was not sought out at all, and that circumstances simply fell out of themselves in such a way that no other name could possibly be given, and it happened precisely like this. Akaky Akakievich was born towards night, if my memory does not fail me, on the twenty-third of March. His late mother, a clerk's wife and a very good woman, arranged, as is proper, to have the child christened. The mother was still lying on the bed facing the door, while on her right hand stood the godfather, a most excellent man, Ivan Ivanovich Yeroshkin, who served as a head of section in the Senate, and the godmother, the wife of a police officer and a woman of rare virtues, Arina Semyonovna Belobryushkova.",
3: "The new mother was offered a choice of any of three names that she cared to pick: Mokkiy, Sossiy, or to name the child after the martyr Khozdazat. {W:“No,”} thought the late lady, {W:“those are all such names.”} To please her they opened the calendar at another place; again three names came up: Trifily, Dula and Varakhasiy. {W:“This is a punishment,”} the old woman said. {W:“What names they all are! I really never heard such names. If it were Varadat or Varukh, but Trifily and Varakhasiy!”} They turned over another page, and out came Pavsikakhiy and Vakhtisiy.",
4: "{W:“Well, I see,”} said the old woman, {W:“that it is plainly his fate. If that is so, then let him be called what his father was called. His father was Akaky, so let the son be Akaky too.”} And that is how Akaky Akakievich came about. The child was christened, whereupon he wept and pulled such a face as if he had a foreboding that he would be a titular councillor. So that is how it all came about. We relate it so that the reader may see for himself that it happened entirely out of necessity and that it was quite impossible to give him another name. When and at what time he entered the department, and who appointed him, nobody could remember.",
5: "However many directors and superiors of every kind came and went, he was seen always in one and the same place, in the same position, in the very same post, the same clerk for copying; so that afterwards people became convinced that he had evidently been born into the world already quite ready-made, in a uniform coat and with a bald patch on his head. In the department no respect was shown him at all. The porters not only did not rise from their seats when he passed, but did not even look at him, as though a mere fly had flown across the waiting room. His superiors treated him with a sort of cold despotism. Some assistant to the head of section would simply shove papers under his nose without even saying “copy this,” or “here is an interesting, pretty little case,” or any such pleasant remark, as is the custom in well-bred offices.",
6: "And he would take it, looking only at the paper, not looking at who had put it there or whether he had any right to. He took it and at once settled down to copy it. The young clerks laughed at him and made jokes about him, as far as chancellery wit would stretch; they told stories invented about him right in front of him, about his landlady, an old woman of seventy, said that she beat him, asked when their wedding would be, and scattered bits of paper on his head, calling it snow. But Akaky Akakievich did not answer a single word to this, as if there were nobody before him; it did not even affect his work: in the midst of all these annoyances he did not make a single mistake in his writing.",
7: "Only if the joke was too unbearable, when they jogged his elbow and kept him from his work, he would say: {A:“Leave me alone, why do you insult me?”} And there was something strange in the words and in the voice in which they were spoken. There was something in it that moved one to pity, so that one young man, newly appointed, who, following the example of the others, had allowed himself to laugh at him, suddenly stopped as if pierced through, and from then on everything seemed to change before him and appear in another light. Some unnatural force pushed him away from the colleagues whom he had met, taking them for decent, polished people. And for a long time afterwards, in the midst of his merriest moments, there would appear before him the short clerk with the little bald patch on his forehead and his piercing words: {A:“Leave me alone, why do you insult me?”}",
8: "And in those piercing words other words rang out: “I am your brother.” And the poor young man covered his face with his hand, and many times in his life he shuddered afterwards, seeing how much inhumanity there is in man, how much savage coarseness is hidden in refined, educated manners, and, O God, even in the man whom the world acknowledges as noble and honourable.",
9: "It would be hard to find anywhere a man who lived so much in his work. It is not enough to say that he served zealously: no, he served with love. There, in that copying, he saw a varied and pleasant world of his own. Delight showed on his face; certain letters were his favourites, and when he reached them he was beside himself: he chuckled and winked and helped along with his lips, so that in his face you could, it seemed, read every letter that his pen was drawing. If rewards had been given in proportion to his zeal, he might, to his own astonishment, even have risen to be a state councillor; but what he earned, as his colleagues the wits put it, was a buckle for his buttonhole and piles in the small of his back. Still, one cannot say that no notice was ever taken of him.",
10: "One director, being a kind man and wishing to reward him for his long service, ordered that he be given something more important than ordinary copying; namely, from a case that was already finished he was told to prepare some communication to another office; the whole business was only to change the heading and to change a few verbs here and there from the first person to the third. This gave him such labour that he broke into a sweat, rubbed his forehead and at last said: {A:“No, better give me something to copy.”} From then on they left him to copy for ever. Outside this copying, nothing seemed to exist for him. He did not think at all about his clothes: his uniform coat was not green but of some reddish, floury colour.",
11: "His collar was narrow and low, so that his neck, though it was not long, coming out of the collar seemed extraordinarily long, like the plaster kittens with nodding heads that Russian foreigners carry about on their heads by the dozen. And something was always sticking to his coat: a bit of hay or some thread; besides, he had a special knack of arriving beneath a window just at the moment when all sorts of rubbish was being thrown out of it, and so he was forever carrying off melon and watermelon rinds and such rubbish on his hat.",
12: "Not once in his life did he pay attention to what goes on and happens every day in the street, which, as is well known, his own brother, the young clerk, always looks at, pushing the penetration of his sharp glance so far that he will notice even whose trouser strap has come unstitched at the bottom on the other side of the pavement, which always brings a sly smile to his face.",
13: "But Akaky Akakievich, if he looked at anything, saw everywhere his own neat lines written out in an even hand, and only if, from who knows where, a horse's muzzle came to rest on his shoulder and blew a whole wind into his cheek through its nostrils, only then did he notice that he was not in the middle of a line but rather in the middle of the street. Coming home, he sat down at the table at once, hurriedly slurped his cabbage soup and ate a piece of beef with onion, not noticing their taste at all, ate it all with the flies and with whatever else God happened to send. Noticing that his stomach had begun to swell, he got up from the table, took out a little bottle of ink and copied papers that he had brought home.",
14: "And if there were none of those, he would make, on purpose and for his own pleasure, a copy for himself, especially if the paper was remarkable not for the beauty of its style but for its address to some new or important person.",
15: "Even at those hours when the grey Petersburg sky quite goes out and all the official people have eaten and dined, each as he could, according to his salary and his own fancy; when everyone has rested after the department's scratching of pens, the running about, their own and other people's necessary tasks and all that a restless man sets himself of his own free will, even more than is needed; when the clerks hurry to give the remaining time over to pleasure: the livelier one dashes to the theatre; another into the street, assigning it to the study of some little hats; another spends the evening on compliments to some pretty girl, the star of a small official circle; another, and this happens most often, simply goes to see a colleague on the third or fourth floor, in two small rooms with an entrance hall or kitchen and some fashionable pretensions, a lamp or some other trifle that cost many sacrifices, renouncing of dinners and outings; in a word, even at the time when all the clerks scatter among their friends' little flats to play whist, sipping tea from glasses with kopeck rusks, drawing smoke from long pipes, telling while the cards are dealt some gossip that has drifted down from high society, which a Russian can never and under no circumstances give up, or even, when there is nothing to talk about, retelling the eternal anecdote about the commandant who was told that the tail of the horse on the Falconet monument had been cut off; in a word, even when everyone is trying to amuse himself, Akaky Akakievich gave himself up to no amusement whatever.",
16: "No one could say that he had ever seen him at any party. When he had written his fill, he went to bed, smiling in advance at the thought of the next day: what would God send him to copy tomorrow? So flowed the peaceful life of a man who, on a salary of four hundred, knew how to be content with his lot, and it might have flowed on to a ripe old age if there were not various calamities strewn along life's road not only for titular, but even for privy, actual, court and every other kind of councillor, even for those who give no counsel to anyone and take none from anyone themselves.",
17: "There is in Petersburg a mighty enemy of all who receive four hundred roubles a year in salary, or thereabouts. This enemy is none other than our northern frost, although they say that it is very healthy. At nine o'clock in the morning, precisely at the hour when the streets are covered with people walking to their departments, it begins to deal such strong and stinging flicks, without distinction, to all noses, that the poor clerks absolutely do not know where to put them. At this time, when even those who hold the highest posts have an ache in the forehead from the frost and tears come to their eyes, the poor titular councillors are sometimes defenceless. All their salvation lies in running, in a skimpy little overcoat, through five or six streets as fast as possible, and then stamping their feet well in the porter's lodge, until all the faculties and talents for official duties that froze on the way have thawed out.",
18: "For some time Akaky Akakievich had begun to feel that he was being baked in a particularly sharp way in the back and shoulder, in spite of his trying to run the lawful distance as fast as he could. At last he wondered whether there might not be some sins in his overcoat. Examining it carefully at home, he discovered that in two or three places, namely on the back and the shoulders, it had become a regular piece of gauze: the cloth was so worn that it let the light through, and the lining had come apart. It must be known that Akaky Akakievich's overcoat had also served as a target of the clerks' jokes; they even took from it the noble name of overcoat and called it a dressing gown. And indeed it was of a strange construction: its collar grew smaller every year, since it went to patch the other parts. The patching did not show a tailor's skill and came out baggy and ugly.",
19: "Seeing what the matter was, Akaky Akakievich decided that the overcoat would have to be taken to Petrovich, the tailor, who lived somewhere on the fourth floor up a back staircase and who, in spite of his one crooked eye and the pockmarks all over his face, was fairly successful at mending clerks' and all other sorts of trousers and tailcoats, of course when he was sober and was not nursing some other scheme in his head. Of this tailor, naturally, there would be no need to say much, but since it is the custom that in a story the character of every person should be fully set out, there is nothing for it, bring us Petrovich here too.",
20: "At first he was called simply Grigory and was a serf of some gentleman; he began to be called Petrovich from the time he received his freedom papers and began to drink rather heavily on every holiday, at first on the great ones and then, without distinction, on all the church ones, wherever a little cross stood in the calendar. In this respect he was faithful to the ways of his grandfathers, and when quarrelling with his wife he called her a worldly woman and a German.",
21: "Since we have already mentioned the wife, two words will have to be said about her too; but unfortunately little was known about her, except perhaps that Petrovich has a wife, who even wears a cap and not a kerchief; but she could not, it seems, boast of beauty; at least, on meeting her, only the Guards soldiers peeped under her cap, twitching a moustache and giving out some peculiar sound.",
}
